[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) calls every report one issue with one fix queued. The reports hold
two problems with two different fixes. The table below is built from the ticket export at render
time, so the counts come from the data, not from this paragraph. Its "no ticket of its own" label
comes from the export, which is older than the ticket filed below.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix does not close #4471. It leaves the <code>hint</code> reports open, because
their dates are wrong for a different reason.
</Callout>

## The two problems

**`parser`: filed as [macyork/mega-what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/macyork/mega-what-it-shows/issues/1).** The shared parser reads every date month first, so a
day-first tenant's dates come out flipped. It reaches every tenant here, on the app, email and
form channels.

**`hint`: filed as [macyork/mega-what-it-shows#2](https://github.com/macyork/mega-what-it-shows/issues/2).** The `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date
field, so people type month first, and the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first.
It reaches only the day-first tenants on `booking-v3`, and only through the form channel. A
change to the parser does not touch it. The fix is in the form.

## How to tell them apart

Use the tenant and the channel, not the subject or the intake tag. The rule is in
`src/lib/kb/causes.ts`:

1. Look up the tenant's locale and form in the tenant directory.
2. A report through the form channel, from a tenant on `booking-v3`, is `hint`.
3. Any other report from a day-first tenant is `parser`.

The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, so a reporter on an `en-US` browser gets
`hint` wherever they work. The "tagged as" column shows where that goes wrong. The subject does
not help either: the same subjects appear under both causes, which is how subject matching in
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) put both into one ticket.

## Still open

The desk read the reports in the note under the table as a one-day move, not a flipped date.
For example, one says the invite showed Tuesday 12 May and the calendar showed Monday 11 May.
A month-first misreading does not explain that. They stay counted under the cause their tenant
gives, and nobody has confirmed the cause. Check them again once the `parser` fix ships.

